  {
    "number": 106757,
    "title": "PR #35568: Fix crash in LatencyHidingScheduler with compute offload",
    "user": "copybara-service[bot]",
    "created_at": "2025-12-19T19:02:53",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-19T20:08:08",
    "body": "PR #35568: Fix crash in LatencyHidingScheduler with compute offload\n\nImported from GitHub PR https://github.com/openxla/xla/pull/35568\n\n📝 Summary of Changes\nWhen running JAX unit tests with compute offload (e.g., test_compute_host_loop), the LatencyHidingScheduler(LHS) crashes with std::out_of_range exceptions when trying to access schedules and pressure states for host computations.\n\nHost computations created during compute offload don't have device schedules (they execute separately on the host CPU), but the scheduler assumed all computations have schedules and pressure states, leading to crashes. So we add defensive checks to skip host computations that don't have schedules or pressure states.\n\n🎯 Justification\nThis CL prevents JAX compute offload code from crashing in LHS when LHS is enabled. Without this fix, any HLO module containing host computations (created during compute offload) would crash during scheduling.\n\n🚀 Kind of Contribution\n 🐛 Bug Fix\n\n📊 Benchmark (for Performance Improvements)\nN/A.\n\n🧪 Unit Tests:\nA new unit test was added: HostOffloadComputationsWithoutSchedule in xla/service/latency_hiding_scheduler_test.cc.\n\n🧪 Execution Tests:\n The fix was verified with JAX end-to-end execution tests. jax/tests/memories_test.py contains compute offload tests in the ComputeOffload test class (e.g.,  test_compute_host_loop) that previously failed due to the scheduler crash. After the fix, these tests pass, confirming the scheduler correctly handles host computations without device schedules.\n\nCopybara import of the project:\n\n--\n244bdc61ee4b8acfbc89e7247421f237cc5838c0 by Jane Liu <janeliu@nvidia.com>:\n\nFix crash in LatencyHidingScheduler with compute offload\n\nMerging this change closes #35568\n\nFUTURE_COPYBARA_INTEGRATE_REVIEW=https://github.com/openxla/xla/pull/35568 from zhenying-liu:lhs-compute-on-fix 244bdc61ee4b8acfbc89e7247421f237cc5838c0\n",
    "html_url": "https://github.com/tensorflow/tensorflow/pull/106757",
    "files": [
      {
        "filename": "third_party/xla/xla/service/latency_hiding_scheduler.cc",
        "additions": 26,
        "deletions": 2
      },
      {
        "filename": "third_party/xla/xla/service/latency_hiding_scheduler_test.cc",
        "additions": 68,
        "deletions": 0
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "number": 106694,
    "title": "[PJRT] Change ExecuteChunk to support operating on only a chunk of the input or output buffer, rather than the whole thing.",
    "user": "copybara-service[bot]",
    "created_at": "2025-12-18T21:59:35",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-19T20:02:48",
    "body": "[PJRT] Change ExecuteChunk to support operating on only a chunk of the input or output buffer, rather than the whole thing.\n\nThis is a key change that should allow us to software pipeline transposes with DMAs: we can divide the transpose up into chunks, where each chunk only has a DMA-sized chunk. We can then handle the transfers for these chunks independently.\n\n* add input_is_global and output_is_global options to ExecuteChunk. If global, we have the entire array, and if local, we have a chunk.\n* identify a subset of the loops that are contiguous (minor most in the contiguous dimensions). Mark these as non-parallel, and arrange that they sort at the end of the loop order.\n* handling negative strides in the input was a source of a fair amount of complexity. To handle these correctly we split the per-chunk offset into a physical offset of a chunk from the pointer the user gives, and the logical location of the start of the iteration space within that chunk.\n",
    "html_url": "https://github.com/tensorflow/tensorflow/pull/106694",
    "files": [
